		<!-- BEAT 05 // INTEGRATION - two paths, and the difference between them is
		     stated rather than implied: one lives inside the turn, the other speaks
		     HTTP. The wording is /hermes/'s and /plugin/'s own, not a second
		     description of the same thing. The two plates are differentiated by
		     TIER - the native lane is the standard plate, the proxy is the hairline
		     one - because the plugin is the lane this page installs and the proxy
		     is the lane that extends it to other callers. -->
		<section class="relative w-full" data-reveal>
			<p class="section-kicker mb-4">05 / INTEGRATION</p>
			<h2 class="type-section mb-6 text-bone-white">Two ways to run it</h2>
			<div class="prose-flow max-w-3xl">
				<p class="type-body text-bone-newsprint">
					Both lanes run the same engine over the same CCR store. One is a native plugin inside
					Hermes, where the interception lives inside the turn; the other is a listener on a port,
					where it can only see traffic that arrives over HTTP.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="mt-12 grid grid-cols-1 items-start gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2">
				<article class="emphasis-standard p-6 sm:p-8">
					<p class="type-meta font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-weathered-teal">
						PATH 01 // NATIVE PLUGIN
					</p>
					<h3 class="type-sub mt-3 text-bone-white">Hermes plugin</h3>
					<div class="prose-flow mt-4">
						<p class="type-body text-bone-newsprint">
							Aphrodite connects to Hermes Agent as a native plugin, not as a proxy you point a
							client at. It is the reason the interception lives inside the turn: tool output never
							hits a wire, so only hook-level registration can catch it before it becomes message
							history - six hooks, thirteen tools, and an optional context engine, every call
							forwarded into the Rust dylib.
						</p>
						<p class="type-body text-bone-newsprint">
							The trade-off is stated plainly: the hooks are Hermes' contract - no other client can
							register them, so this lane works with Hermes only.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-2 border-t border-oxblood-dark pt-4">
						<a class="type-code font-bold text-stamped-canary hover:underline" href={Routes.Hermes}>
							THE INTEGRATION &rarr;
						</a>
						<a class="type-code font-bold text-stamped-canary hover:underline" href={Routes.Plugin}>
							THE PLUGIN &rarr;
						</a>
					</div>
				</article>
				<article class="emphasis-quiet p-6 sm:p-8">
					<p class="type-meta font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-zinc-dust">
						PATH 02 // OPENAI-COMPATIBLE PROXY
					</p>
					<h3 class="type-sub mt-3 text-bone-white">Standalone proxy</h3>
					<div class="prose-flow mt-4">
						<p class="type-body text-bone-newsprint">
							The plugin already spawns both listeners, so this lane runs without extra setup:
							point <code class="type-code text-weathered-teal">base_url</code> at
							<code class="type-code text-weathered-teal">:9798</code> and any OpenAI-compatible
							client gets the same CCR compression over plain HTTP. That is the agent-to-agent
							path - a second agent, a script, or an external tool can converse through the same
							compressed channel, share the persistent SQLite store (which survives a restart;
							the plugin lane's inline store is session-scoped), and call the tool relay to
							retrieve or create CCR entries.
						</p>
						<p class="type-body text-bone-newsprint">
							The limit is real too: the proxy only sees HTTP traffic - no tool-output interception,
							no terminal compression, no directives, no context engine, and SSE streams pass
							through uncompressed. Because of that the proxy extends the stack to other callers;
							it does not replace the hooks inside Hermes.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-2 border-t border-oxblood-dark pt-4">
						<a class="type-code font-bold text-stamped-canary hover:underline" href={Anchor(Routes.Setup, "proxy")}>
							THE PROXY LANE &rarr;
						</a>
					</div>
				</article>
			</div>
		</section>

		<!-- BEAT 06 // INSTALLATION - one focused procedural panel: what you need,
