					</li>
					<li class="type-body text-bone-newsprint">
						<code class="type-code text-bone-white">bash download.sh</code> - run from inside
						<code class="type-code text-weathered-teal">~/.hermes/plugins/aphrodite</code>. It
						fetches the binary and the dylib into
						<code class="type-code text-weathered-teal">~/.hermes/aphrodite/binaries/</code>,
						validated against the in-tree
						<code class="type-code text-weathered-teal">SHA256SUMS.txt</code> and magic-byte
						checked. On Windows: <code class="type-code text-weathered-teal">pwsh ./download.ps1</code>.
					</li>
					<li class="type-body text-bone-newsprint">
						<code class="type-code text-bone-white">hermes</code> - start it.
					</li>
				</ol>
				<p class="type-meta mt-8 font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-stamped-canary">EXPECTED RESULT</p>
				<div class="prose-flow mt-3">
					<p class="type-body text-bone-newsprint">
						Reviewed pinned checkout of the catalog entry, binaries fetched + SHA-256 verified,
						plugin enabled. Start <code class="type-code text-weathered-teal">hermes</code> and the
						plugin logs that Aphrodite is enabled.
					</p>
					<p class="type-body text-bone-newsprint">
						Two proxy listeners come up with it:
						<code class="type-code text-weathered-teal">:9797</code> (the cache proxy, threshold
						2048 bytes by default) and <code class="type-code text-weathered-teal">:9798</code>
						(the token proxy, threshold 256 bytes by default).
					</p>
					<p class="type-caption text-zinc-dust">
						If the binaries are missing, the plugin logs the setup command and stays disabled until
						you run it.
					</p>
				</div>
